 Multi-turn dialogue with Large Language Models (LLMs) suffers from progressive attention decay toward system prompt goal tokens. On Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, the Goal Attention Ratio (GAR) declines by 70.8\% between early and mid-conversation turns, yet linear probes recover goal content from the residual stream with AUC\,=\,0.98, indicating that goal information persists but the attention mechanism fails to route it. We introduce ThreadGate, which modulates the residual stream with a per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gate ($x_ {l+1} = x_ l + g_ l \cdot f_ l(x_ l)$), trained jointly with next-token prediction (NTP) and an auxiliary GAR loss. A five-way comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct reveals that training succeeds (teacher-forced GAR\,=\,0.50) but generalization fails: under autoregressive generation, GAR drops to 0.015--0.025 across all fine-tuned variants, a 20--33$\times$ gap. Gating contributes zero measurable benefit, with only 0.75 percentage points separating all four fine-tuned variants. A PCGrad experiment establishes that gradient direction conflict is neither necessary nor sufficient for the failure. The failure traces to exposure bias: attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text, and the NTP objective's token-level self-recovery does not extend to auxiliary attention losses. These findings provide the first empirical characterization of how exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary objective generalization while leaving the primary objective intact.
 \end{abstract} introduction.tex \section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction} Multi-turn dialogue is the dominant mode of interaction with large language models (LLMs), and system prompts that specify task goals form the foundation of instruction following. As conversations grow longer, however, models progressively lose track of these goal specifications. \citet{dongre2026attention} quantified this degradation across 12 models: the Goal Attention Ratio (GAR), which measures how much attention the model allocates to goal tokens in the system prompt, declines by 27--48\% between early and mid-conversation turns. We replicate and extend this finding on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, observing a 70.8\% relative decline in GAR from 0.0418 at turn~ 1 to 0.0122 at mid-conversation. This attention decay represents a systematic failure of multi-turn models to maintain task coherence, and it grows worse with each additional turn. A striking aspect of this degradation is what it does \emph{not} affect. \citet{dongre2026attention} showed that goal-relevant information persists in the residual stream throughout the conversation: linear discriminant analysis probes recover goal content with AUC\,=\,0.98 even at late turns where attention to goal tokens has collapsed. The information is present in the hidden states, but the attention mechanism fails to route it toward the output computation. This dissociation between information availability and information utilization suggests a natural intervention: if the residual stream already carries goal information, a learnable gate on the residual pathway could amplify or attenuate layer contributions to recover goal attention. This reasoning motivates ThreadGate, which modulates each residual update with a content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gate: $x_ {l+1} = x_ l + g_ l \cdot f_ l(x_ l)$, where $g_ l$ is produced by a two-layer network conditioned on the hidden state and turn features, inspired by the residual scaling idea of ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}. \begin{figure* }[ t ]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[ width=\textwidth]{fig1_ overview.pdf}
 \caption{Overview of ThreadGate and its failure mode. (Left) ThreadGate adds per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates to the residual stream, trained jointly with an auxiliary GAR loss that targets goal attention. (Right) Under teacher-forced evaluation, GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants; under autoregressive generation, it drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap. The five-way comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) shows that no intervention provides measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning.}
 \label{fig:overview}
 \end{figure* } ThreadGate combines per-layer MLP gate networks with a joint training objective: next-token prediction (NTP), an auxiliary GAR loss that pushes attention toward goal tokens via an MSE target ($\tau = 0.5$), and a gate diversity loss that prevents all gates from collapsing to a single value (\S\ref{sec:method}). The final loss formulation emerged from seven design iterations (v1 through v7) spanning four loss families (cumulative, maximize, smooth saturation, and MSE target); only the MSE target with gate diversity converged stably. To isolate the contributions of gating and gradient conflict, we evaluate five configurations: ThreadGate (turn-conditioned gates), +PCGrad (ThreadGate with gradient conflict elimination), Uniform Gate (content-only gates), LoRA-only (gates fixed at 1.0), and the unmodified Base model. This factorial design supports four targeted comparisons that separate gradient conflict, turn conditioning, gating as a mechanism, and fine-tuning itself (\S\ref{sec:experiments}). Training succeeds: teacher-forced GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants. The failure emerges under autoregressive generation, where GAR-ours drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap that persists across all training strategies. All four fine-tuned variants achieve indistinguishable performance (0.75\,pp spread on GAR-ours, identical PPL at 2.78), confirming that gating contributes zero measurable benefit. A PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad} experiment eliminates gradient direction conflict without altering any outcome, establishing that conflict is neither necessary nor sufficient for the failure. The root failure is exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled}: attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text (Figure~ \ref{fig:overview}). Our contributions: (1)~ the first empirical characterization of how exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary attention loss generalization (20--33$\times$ gap across all training strategies) while leaving NTP robust, showing that token-level self-recovery \citep{he2019exposure} does not extend to auxiliary objectives; (2)~ a PCGrad causal exclusion establishing that gradient direction conflict is neither necessary nor sufficient for the failure (\S\ref{sec:analysis}); (3)~ ThreadGate and a five-way comparison providing a principled negative baseline, with seven loss formulations and four training configurations ruled out (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}). background.tex \section{Background}\label{sec:background} \paragraph{Goal Attention Ratio.}
 Multi-turn instruction-following models receive a system prompt that specifies the task objective. We denote the token subsequence corresponding to the task specification within this system prompt as the \emph{goal tokens} $\mathcal{G}$, excluding formatting markers and role tags. \citet{dongre2026attention} introduced the Goal Attention Ratio (GAR) to quantify how much attention a model allocates to these goal tokens during response generation. Given a model with $L$ layers and $H$ attention heads, GAR at conversation turn $\tau$ is
 \begin{equation}\label{eq:gar}
 \text{GAR}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H \cdot |\mathcal{R} \tau|} \sum {\ell=1}^{L} \sum_ {h=1}^{H} \sum_ {i \in \mathcal{R} \tau} \sum {j \in \mathcal{G}} A_ {i,j}^{(\ell,h)},
 \end{equation}
 where $\mathcal{R} \tau$ is the set of response tokens at turn $\tau$ and $A {i,j}^{(\ell,h)}$ is the attention weight from token $i$ to token $j$ at layer $\ell$, head $h$. This definition averages attention to goal tokens over all response positions, all layers, and all heads. \paragraph{Two GAR variants.}
 We distinguish two measurement variants throughout this paper. \textbf{GAR-Dongre} refers to the original definition in Equation~ \ref{eq:gar}, which averages over the full response token set $\mathcal{R} \tau$. \textbf{GAR-ours} restricts the measurement to the \emph{last token} of each assistant turn:\begin{equation}\label{eq:gar-ours}\text{GAR\text{-}ours}(\tau) = \frac{1}{L \cdot H} \sum {\ell=1}^{L} \sum_ {h=1}^{H} \sum_ {j \in \mathcal{G}} A_ {t_ \tau,\, j}^{(\ell,h)},
 \end{equation}
 where $t_ \tau$ is the index of the last token in the assistant response at turn $\tau$. The last token is the position at which the model conditions its next generation step, so attention patterns at this position most directly reflect the information the model draws upon for continuation. We use GAR-ours as the optimization target for the auxiliary loss $\mathcal{L}_ \text{GAR}$ (\S\ref{sec:loss}), while GAR-Dongre provides a complementary aggregate measure for comparison. \paragraph{Residual stream persistence.}
 \citet{dongre2026attention} showed that goal-relevant information persists in the residual stream across conversation turns: linear discriminant analysis probes achieve AUC\,=\,0.98 for detecting goal content even at late turns where GAR has declined substantially. The information remains present in the hidden states, but the attention mechanism ceases to route it toward the output computation. This dissociation between information availability and information utilization motivates an intervention that targets the residual pathway directly. \paragraph{Problem statement.}
 GAR decays across conversation turns while goal information persists in the residual stream. We ask whether learnable residual gates can recover goal attention without degrading language modeling quality. method.tex \section{Method}\label{sec:method} \subsection{ThreadGate Architecture} Inspired by the residual scaling idea of ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}, ThreadGate modulates the residual stream with a content-and-turn-conditioned gate at each transformer layer. For a model with $L$ layers, the gated residual update at layer $l$ is
 \begin{equation}
 x_ {l+1} = x_ l + g_ l \cdot f_ l(x_ l),
 \end{equation}
 where $f_ l$ denotes the sublayer computation (self-attention or feed-forward network). Unlike ReZero's single scalar multiplier, $g_ l$ is produced by a two-layer MLP gate network that conditions on both the hidden state and turn-level features:
 \begin{equation}\label{eq:gate}
 g_ l = \sigma\!\bigl(W_ 2\,\text{ReLU}(W_ 1\,[ \text{proj}(x_ l);\;\text{emb}(t_ d, t_ i)] + b_ 1) + b_ 2\bigr),
 \end{equation}
 where $\text{proj}(\cdot)$ is a linear projection from the hidden dimension to 16 units, and $\text{emb}(t_ d, t_ i)$ is a 16-dimensional turn embedding. The turn embedding encodes two features: \emph{turn distance} $t_ d$, the number of turns elapsed since the system prompt, and \emph{turn index} $t_ i$, the absolute position of the current turn in the conversation. Both features are embedded via learned lookup tables and concatenated. The projection and turn embedding are concatenated into a 32-dimensional input, passed through a hidden layer of 64 units with ReLU activation, and reduced to a scalar by the output layer. When $g_ l \to 0$, the sublayer output is fully suppressed and the layer reduces to an identity mapping; when $g_ l \to 1$, the full sublayer output passes through the residual stream. Each of the 28 transformer layers in Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct receives its own independent gate network, so the model learns a per-layer, per-token, turn-conditioned gating profile. The gate network outputs are initialized near 0.82 (by biasing the output layer) to preserve most of the pretrained computation at the start of fine-tuning. We apply a learning rate multiplier of 10 to all gate network parameters relative to the base learning rate. \subsection{Training Objective}\label{sec:loss} The training objective combines three terms:
 \begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L} \text{NTP} + \lambda \text{GAR}\,\mathcal{L} \text{GAR} + \lambda \text{div}\,\mathcal{L} \text{div}.\end{equation}$\mathcal{L} \text{NTP}$ is the standard next-token prediction cross-entropy loss. The two auxiliary losses target the gating mechanism directly. The Goal Attention Ratio (GAR) loss encourages the model to maintain a target level of attention to goal-relevant tokens. We define it as
 \begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L}_ \text{GAR} = \text{MSE}(\text{GAR}, \tau),
 \end{equation}
 where $\tau = 0.5$ is the target ratio. GAR is computed under teacher-forced context and measured only at the last token position of each assistant turn (the GAR-ours variant defined in \S\ref{sec:background}), following the observation of \citet{dongre2026attention} that attention to goal tokens is most informative at turn boundaries. This loss steers the attention distribution toward a balanced allocation between goal tokens and other context. The gate diversity loss prevents all gates from collapsing to a single value:
 \begin{equation}
 \mathcal{L} \text{div} = \text{ReLU}(\sigma \text{min} - \text{std}(\mathbf{g})),
 \end{equation}
 where $\mathbf{g} = [ g_ 1, \ldots, g_ L]$ is the vector of gate values across all layers and $\sigma_ \text{min}$ is a minimum standard deviation threshold. Without this term, the gates can converge to a uniform value across layers, which collapses the per-layer conditioning to a single global scalar and eliminates any advantage over a shared gate. Both auxiliary loss weights are set to $\lambda_ \text{GAR} = \lambda_ \text{div} = 1.0$. The final loss formulation emerged from seven design iterations (v1 through v7) that explored cumulative, maximization, smooth saturation, and MSE-based objectives. The MSE target combined with gate diversity was the only formulation that converged stably; other variants either diverged or drove all gates to the same value. A detailed comparison of all loss variants is provided in Appendix~ B. The four-way ablation design and experimental setup are described in \S\ref{sec:experiments}. experiments.tex \section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments} \subsection{Setup} We fine-tune Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct on 8K multi-turn conversations from the UltraChat training split, a dataset of naturalistic dialogues across diverse topics. LoRA adapters (rank 8) are applied to all linear layers. The combined loss $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L} \text{NTP} + \lambda \text{GAR}\mathcal{L} \text{GAR} + \lambda \text{div}\mathcal{L} \text{div}$ uses $\lambda \text{GAR} = \lambda_ \text{div} = 1.0$ with MSE target $\tau = 0.5$ (\S\ref{sec:loss}). We train for 3 epochs with AdamW (lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$, batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8, seed 42); each variant requires approximately 6 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (102\,GB). Full hyperparameters are provided in Appendix~ A. To isolate the contributions of gating and gradient conflict, we evaluate five configurations. \textbf{ThreadGate} is the full system: LoRA adapters on all linear layers, 28 independent content-and-turn-conditioned gate networks (Eq.~ \ref{eq:gate}), and the complete three-term loss. \textbf{\;+\,PCGrad} adds PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad} to ThreadGate, projecting conflicting NTP and GAR gradients onto each other's normal planes; the architecture and loss are identical to ThreadGate. \textbf{Uniform Gate} retains 28 per-layer gate networks but replaces the turn embedding with a learned constant vector, removing turn conditioning while retaining content-dependent gating. \textbf{LoRA-only} fixes all gates at 1.0, trained with $\mathcal{L} \text{NTP} + \mathcal{L} \text{GAR}$ ($\lambda_ \text{GAR} = 1.0$) but without $\mathcal{L}_ \text{div}$. \textbf{Base} is the pretrained model without fine-tuning. ThreadGate vs.\ +PCGrad isolates gradient direction conflict; ThreadGate vs.\ Uniform Gate isolates turn conditioning; ThreadGate vs.\ LoRA-only isolates gating; LoRA-only vs.\ Base isolates fine-tuning. All fine-tuned variants share training data, LoRA configuration, optimizer settings, and random seed. Each configuration is evaluated under two teacher-forced protocols. \emph{IID evaluation} computes metrics on UltraChat text from the same distribution as training. \emph{Held-out evaluation} uses 50 non-overlapping conversations to test within-distribution generalization. We report three metrics: GAR-ours (attention to goal tokens at the last token of each assistant turn, as defined in \S\ref{sec:background}), GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention} (attention to goal tokens averaged across all response tokens, all layers, and all heads), and perplexity (PPL). \subsection{Main Results} Table~ \ref{tab:four-way} presents the five-way comparison. The central finding is that all four fine-tuned variants converge to nearly identical performance. GAR-ours spans only 0.75 percentage points (pp) across ThreadGate (0.50), +PCGrad (0.50), Uniform Gate (0.51), and LoRA-only (0.50), and PPL is 2.78 for all four on IID evaluation. Neither per-layer gating (ThreadGate), elimination of gradient direction conflict (+PCGrad), nor content-only gating (Uniform Gate) provides any measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning. The Base model confirms that LoRA fine-tuning is the operative variable: GAR-ours jumps from 0.01 to ${\sim}$0.50, while PPL improves from 4.23 to 2.78. Since all four variants share the same GAR loss ($\tau = 0.5$), convergence near the target is expected and does not demonstrate gating benefit. GAR-Dongre remains uniformly low (0.01--0.02) across all conditions, consistent with \citet{dongre2026attention}. The teacher-forced GAR-ours of 0.50--0.51 stands in sharp contrast to autoregressive generation, where GAR-ours drops to 0.015--0.025 across all fine-tuned variants, a 20--33$\times$ gap. This gap is universal: it persists across all training strategies---gating, gradient conflict elimination, and LoRA-only fine-tuning alike. We analyze this distribution shift in \S\ref{sec:analysis}. \begin{table* }[ t ]
