 \end{figure} analysis.tex \section{Analysis}\label{sec:analysis} The four-way ablation in \S\ref{sec:experiments} established that gating produces no measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning. This section investigates why. We show that the learned gates exhibit behavior opposite to the design intent (\S\ref{subsec:gate-conditioning}), use a causal experiment to rule out gradient conflict as the explanation (\S\ref{subsec:pcgrad}), and identify distribution shift as the actual failure mode (\S\ref{subsec:dist-shift}). \subsection{Gate Turn Conditioning}\label{subsec:gate-conditioning} The design intent of ThreadGate is to modulate the residual stream more aggressively at late conversation turns, where GAR naturally decays (\S\ref{sec:background}). We test whether the learned gates achieve this by comparing gate values at turn 1 (early) and turn 21 (late) across all 28 layers of the trained model. The gates do the opposite. In 27 of 28 layers, the gate value at turn 1 is lower than at turn 21 (Wilcoxon signed-rank test: $p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$, effect size $r = 0.975$). At layer 23, the gate is 0.769 at turn 1 and 0.979 at turn 21; at layer 14, it is 0.747 at turn 1 and 0.928 at turn 21. The pattern is consistent: gates suppress the residual stream more at early turns (where GAR is already high) and become nearly passive at late turns (where intervention is most needed). This inversion has a straightforward explanation. NTP loss pressure is highest at late turns, where the model must predict tokens conditioned on a long context. The NTP gradient pushes gates toward 1.0 to preserve the full sublayer computation, and this pressure is strongest precisely where GAR-based attenuation would be most useful. The resulting inference-time gate values range from 0.93 to 0.98 (2--7\% attenuation), far below what meaningful modulation would require, explaining the convergence with LoRA-only in Table~ \ref{tab:four-way}. Figure~ \ref{fig:gate-shift}(a) visualizes the per-layer gate difference. \begin{figure}[ t ]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[ width=\columnwidth]{fig3_ gate_ shift.pdf}
 \caption{(a) Gate turn conditioning: per-layer gate values at turn 1 vs turn~ 21. In 27/28 layers, gates approach 1.0 at late turns, inverting the design intent ($p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$). (b)~ Distribution shift: the 20--34$\times$ GAR gap persists across all five variants (TG, +PCGrad, UG, LoRA-only, Base), confirming that the failure is universal.}
 \label{fig:gate-shift}
 \end{figure} \subsection{PCGrad Causal Exclusion}\label{subsec:pcgrad} The NTP and GAR gradients point in opposing directions across 68--79\% of layers throughout training, with mean cosine similarity ranging from $-$0.089 (epoch 0) to $-$0.042 (epoch 2). The NTP gradient magnitude dominates by 7.5--14.7$\times$. This raises a natural hypothesis: perhaps opposing gradient directions prevent the GAR objective from converging. If so, eliminating direction conflict should prevent the collapse. We test this using PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, which projects the gradient of one task onto the normal plane of the other whenever two task gradients conflict. We apply PCGrad to gate parameters only, scoping our claim to direction conflict; magnitude imbalance \citep{chen2018gradnorm} and Nash equilibrium violations \citep{navon2022nash} remain untested. Eliminating direction conflict via PCGrad shifts cosine similarity from consistently negative ($-$0.089) to oscillating around zero ($-$0.095 to $+$0.090), yet all outcomes remain unchanged: IID GAR-ours (0.50 vs 0.50), PPL (2.780 vs 2.784), and autoregressive GAR (0.015 vs 0.016) are indistinguishable (Table~ \ref{tab:four-way}). $\mathcal{L} \text{GAR}$ follows a nearly identical training trajectory and collapses in the same manner (Figure~\ref{fig:training-pcgrad}b). The causal exclusion follows by \emph{modus tollens}: if direction conflict causes the failure, removing it should prevent it; PCGrad removes it; the failure persists; therefore direction conflict is not the cause. Direction conflict is neither necessary (removing it does not rescue GAR) nor sufficient ($\mathcal{L} \text{GAR}$ converges during training despite persistent conflict). The 7.5--14.7$\times$ NTP magnitude dominance renders directional interference inconsequential. \begin{figure}[ t ]
 \centering
 \includegraphics[ width=\columnwidth]{fig2_ training_ pcgrad.pdf}
 \caption{(a)~ Gradient cosine similarity between NTP and GAR objectives. v7 (ThreadGate) remains consistently negative; v9 (+PCGrad) oscillates around zero. (b)~ IID evaluation outcomes are indistinguishable: eliminating direction conflict changes nothing.}
 \label{fig:training-pcgrad}
 \end{figure} \subsection{Distribution Shift Analysis}\label{subsec:dist-shift} The preceding subsections explain why the gating mechanism is ineffective during training. This subsection addresses why the learned GAR improvements fail to transfer from training to inference. Recall from \S\ref{sec:experiments} that teacher-forced GAR-ours reaches 0.50 for all fine-tuned variants, while autoregressive GAR-ours drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap that persists across all training strategies. Under same-distribution evaluation (teacher-forced UltraChat text), GAR-ours is approximately 0.50, confirming that the model has learned the intended attention pattern on the training distribution. Under cross-distribution evaluation (model-generated text), GAR-ours collapses universally. The training itself is sound; the failure is one of generalization. GAR-Dongre provides a revealing control. Across both evaluation conditions, GAR-Dongre remains uniformly low (0.01--0.02) with no distribution gap. The distribution shift selectively destroys the concentrated attention pattern captured by GAR-ours (last-token, summary-position attention) while leaving the diffuse attention pattern captured by GAR-Dongre (averaged across all positions) unaffected. This selectivity implies that the distribution shift disrupts a specific learned routing behavior rather than attention computation in general. The mechanism connecting training regime to distribution shift is exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled}. During training, the model conditions each token prediction on ground-truth prefixes from UltraChat. At inference, it conditions on its own prior outputs. The attention patterns optimized for the former distribution do not transfer to the latter, because the statistical properties of model-generated text diverge from those of human-written reference text. The 2--7\% gate attenuation documented in \S\ref{subsec:gate-conditioning} is far too weak to compensate for this distributional mismatch. \citet{he2019exposure} showed that language models exhibit token-level self-recovery from exposure bias: accumulated errors in autoregressive generation do not degrade perplexity as severely as worst-case analysis predicts. Our results are consistent with this finding at the token level, as PPL remains normal across all conditions (Table~ \ref{tab:four-way}). The critical distinction is between primary and auxiliary objectives. The NTP objective is robust to exposure bias because next-token prediction is the same task at both training and inference time. The GAR auxiliary objective is fragile because it optimizes an attention pattern under teacher-forced context that has no counterpart in autoregressive generation. Primary-task self-recovery does not extend to auxiliary objectives that depend on distributional properties of the input prefix. Figure~ \ref{fig:gate-shift}(b) summarizes the four-model GAR comparison across evaluation conditions. related_ work.tex \section{Related Work}\label{sec:related} \paragraph{Multi-turn attention degradation.}
 Recent work has established that LLMs systematically degrade across conversation turns. \citet{levy2025lost} showed that model performance drops on tasks placed in later turns, even when the tasks are identical to earlier ones. \citet{liu2026intent} traced a related failure to intent mismatch, where models progressively shift from the user's original intent as dialogue extends. MT-Bench-101 \citep{bai2024mtbench101} provides fine-grained multi-turn evaluation across 1388 turn-level instances, revealing that capability varies substantially across task categories. \citet{dongre2026attention} offered a mechanistic account: they defined the Goal Attention Ratio (GAR) and showed that attention to system-prompt goal tokens decays by 27--48\% across turns in 12 models, while residual stream probes retain goal information with AUC\,=\,0.98. We observe a steeper 70.8\% decline on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct (\S\ref{sec:experiments}). This dissociation between attention collapse and residual persistence motivates our gating intervention, and our analysis reveals why such interventions fail to transfer from training to inference. \paragraph{Residual gating and adaptive depth.}
 Controlling computation flow through residual connections has a rich history. ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero} showed that initializing residual multipliers at zero enables stable training of very deep networks, establishing the principle that residual scaling can shape optimization dynamics. Subsequent work extended this idea to variable-depth inference: LayerDrop \citep{fan2020layerdrop} applies structured dropout to entire layers during training, Mixture-of-Depths \citep{raposo2024mixture} routes tokens past layers via learned per-token decisions, FlexiDepth \citep{luo2025flexidepth} adds adaptive layer-skipping to pretrained LLMs, and CALM \citep{schuster2022calm} implements early exit based on confidence scores. These methods share the insight that not all layers contribute equally to every input, but they target computational efficiency or training stability. ThreadGate applies residual gating to a different objective: recovering attention patterns in multi-turn dialogue, a setting where adaptive depth methods have not been evaluated. \paragraph{Attention manipulation.}
 StreamingLLM \citep{xiao2024streaming} identified attention sinks and proposed anchor token retention; \citet{ko2024found} showed positional bias against middle-positioned information; \citet{qiu2025gated} replaced softmax with a learned gate for sparse attention. These approaches intervene inside the attention mechanism, while ThreadGate operates on the residual stream. Our results suggest that neither pathway alone overcomes the distributional gap between teacher-forced training and autoregressive inference. \paragraph{Multi-task gradient conflict.}
 GradNorm \citep{chen2018gradnorm}, PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, CAGrad \citep{liu2021cagrad}, and Nash-MTL \citep{navon2022nash} treat gradient conflict between competing losses as a root cause to be resolved through better optimization. We repurpose PCGrad as a causal analysis tool: eliminating directional conflict between NTP and GAR gradients does not improve GAR convergence, establishing gradient conflict as a symptom rather than the cause. \paragraph{Exposure bias.}
 Teacher forcing creates a distributional mismatch between training and inference that compounds over sequence length. Scheduled Sampling \citep{bengio2015scheduled} introduced curriculum-based mixing of ground-truth and model-generated tokens. MIXER \citep{ranzato2016sequence} combined token-level and sequence-level objectives, and Professor Forcing \citep{lamb2016professor} used adversarial regularization to align hidden state distributions under both regimes. \citet{he2019exposure} showed that autoregressive models exhibit self-recovery from exposure bias at the token prediction level. SEARNN \citep{leblond2018searnn} proposed locally recurrent search-based training to avoid the mismatch entirely. This literature focuses on generation quality degradation in primary objectives. Our work identifies a distinct failure mode: auxiliary attention losses that converge under teacher forcing fail completely under autoregressive generation, even when NTP remains robust, because token-level self-recovery does not extend to distributional properties of attention. discussion.tex \section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion} The failure of ThreadGate traces to an incompatibility between teacher-forcing and GAR-family auxiliary attention losses, not to the gating architecture itself. Under teacher-forcing, $\mathcal{L} \text{GAR}$ optimizes attention patterns conditioned on ground-truth prefixes that never appear at inference time. The gate parameters, subject to NTP gradient pressure that grows from 7.5$\times$ to 14.7$\times$ the GAR gradient magnitude (\S\ref{subsec:pcgrad}), converge toward 1.0 and become passive. The resulting 2--7\% residual attenuation cannot compensate for the distributional mismatch between teacher-forced and autoregressive contexts, as the 20--33$\times$ GAR gap confirms (\S\ref{subsec:dist-shift}). Stronger gating (lower initial values, higher $\lambda \text{GAR}$) would degrade NTP performance, while weaker gating is already ineffective. This dilemma is structural: we conjecture that lightweight residual gating interventions trained jointly with NTP under teacher-forcing face the same pressure toward passivity. Four directions follow from the experimental evidence. First, \textbf{inference-time attention intervention} sidesteps the training generalization problem entirely by rescaling or pruning attention heads at decoding time, operating on the actual autoregressive distribution rather than a teacher-forced proxy. Second, a \textbf{decoupled goal-processing pathway}, analogous to adapter modules but dedicated to goal tokens, could maintain goal information through a channel that NTP gradients do not dominate. Third, \textbf{GAR-aware decoding} would incorporate goal attention into the sampling or reranking stage rather than the training loss, avoiding exposure bias by construction. Fourth, \textbf{exposure bias mitigation for auxiliary losses} remains a viable direction: scheduled sampling \citep{bengio2015scheduled} or Professor Forcing \citep{lamb2016professor} applied specifically to $\mathcal{L}_ \text{GAR}$ could reduce the distributional mismatch that destroys GAR transfer. Our v8 scheduled sampling experiment was invalidated by an implementation error; the approach itself warrants further study. Our findings converge with \citet{dongre2026attention}, who showed that goal information persists in the residual stream but attention fails to route it. If goal re-injection were attempted, the injected signal would likely face the same distributional mismatch, suggesting that lightweight forward-pass modifications may face a similar obstacle. We scope our claims to lightweight residual gating with GAR auxiliary losses; more heavyweight interventions (full head retraining, separate goal encoders, RL from attention feedback) fall outside this investigation. The PCGrad exclusion establishes that gradient direction conflict is neither necessary nor sufficient for the failure---the 7.5--14.7$\times$ NTP magnitude dominance renders directional interference inconsequential. Magnitude imbalance \citep{chen2018gradnorm} and Nash equilibrium violations \citep{navon2022nash} remain untested. All experiments use Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct on UltraChat; cross-model generalization remains to be established (\S\ref{sec:limitations}). \paragraph{Implications for practitioners.} These results caution against auxiliary attention losses under standard teacher-forced training: practitioners should prefer inference-time attention interventions or apply exposure bias mitigation specifically to auxiliary objectives. conclusion.tex \section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion} We investigated whether lightweight residual gating can recover goal attention in multi-turn dialogue. ThreadGate applies per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates to the residual stream, trained jointly with an auxiliary Goal Attention Ratio (GAR) loss that targets attention to system prompt goal tokens. Training succeeds: teacher-forced GAR reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants. The failure emerges at inference time. Under autoregressive generation, GAR drops to 0.015--0.025 across all variants, a 20--33$\times$ gap that no training strategy bridges. A five-way comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) confirms that gating contributes zero measurable benefit, with GAR-ours varying by only 0.75 percentage points across four fine-tuned variants. A PCGrad experiment establishes that gradient direction conflict is neither necessary nor sufficient for the failure. The root cause is exposure bias. Attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text, and the primary NTP objective's self-recovery from exposure bias does not extend to auxiliary attention losses. These results indicate that auxiliary attention losses face a generalization barrier under the conditions tested (single model, single dataset, GAR-family losses). Future work should explore inference-time attention intervention or exposure bias mitigation strategies that operate on the autoregressive distribution directly. limitations.tex \section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations} Our experiments use a single random seed (seed\,=\,42). While the four-way convergence across ThreadGate, Uniform Gate, and LoRA-only provides indirect evidence of robustness (three independent configurations reaching indistinguishable performance is unlikely to result from seed-specific artifacts), multi-seed validation would strengthen this conclusion. We test only one type of auxiliary attention loss (GAR with MSE target $\tau = 0.5$). Other attention steering objectives, such as head-specific or layer-specific attention losses, may exhibit different sensitivity to exposure bias. Our negative result applies to the GAR family; it does not rule out all auxiliary attention interventions. Training uses 8K conversations from UltraChat. Larger-scale training could alter gate dynamics, particularly if the model encounters sufficient distributional diversity to reduce the training-inference mismatch. All experiments use a single model family (Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct). Preliminary cross-model experiments on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct are ongoing; until those results are available, our conclusions may not generalize across architectures. The PCGrad causal exclusion addresses gradient direction conflict only. Magnitude imbalance (GradNorm; \citealp{chen2018gradnorm}) and Nash equilibrium approaches (Nash-MTL; \citealp{navon2022nash}) remain untested and could reveal additional failure modes or interaction effects. All three fine-tuned variants share the same GAR auxiliary loss. This design isolates the gating mechanism but does not test whether gating could be effective without an auxiliary attention loss or under a different training objective. 审查重点 1.  数字一致性 ：所有章节中的 GAR 数字（0.50, 0.015-0.025, 20-33×）、PPL（2.78/4.23）是否一致 2.  五路比较完整性 ：abstract/intro/experiments/analysis/discussion/conclusion 是否全部更新为 